  "body": "### Summary\n\nAs a contributor, I want `URLSessionTransportTests.silentServerTimesOut()` to be deterministic on Linux. It failed once, on a docs-only PR, and a red HFRKit job blocks every merge.\n\n### Observed\n\n- PR #1082 (a docs-only ledger fold), job « HFRKit tests (Linux) » 134459613258, 2026-11-17 23:50 UTC: `URLSessionTransportTests.swift:100:15` expected `NetworkError.timedOut`, but `transport(code: -1011)` was thrown. The other 2,111 tests passed. Re-run once, as the flake rule allows.\n- The test was added by #1009 (#683, the 10 s idle limits and the Linux idle watcher, merged 2026-11-17 as `d378e6ed`).\n- `-1011` is `NSURLErrorBadServerResponse`. On Linux, swift-corelibs-foundation seems to report the silent server's dropped connection as a bad response before (or instead of) the idle watcher's timeout: a race between the watcher and the server closing. **To verify; do not assume.**\n\n### Scope\n\n1. Reproduce: run the test in a loop on Linux (`swift test --filter silentServerTimesOut` × 50, under parallel load) and record the failure rate.\n2. Find the race. Who closes first, the loopback test server or the idle watcher? Does the transport map `-1011` after a watcher-initiated cancel?\n3. Fix the cause:\n   - in the transport (map a failure that follows the idle deadline to `.timedOut`), if that is the real behaviour we want;\n   - or in the test server, keeping the connection open past the idle limit.\n   **Never loosen the expectation and never add a sleep** (AGENTS.md rules).\n4. Check that the macOS run keeps passing.\n\n### Acceptance criteria\n\n- 200 consecutive Linux runs pass, under parallel load.\n- A test pins the mapping you choose.\n\nRefs #683, #1009.\n\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
